+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

You may use OpenAI commercial services or misc/llama.cpp, for the last
one you should add into ~/.codex/config.toml:

	model_provider = "local"
	model = "local"
	web_search = "disabled"

	[model_providers.local]
	name = "llama-server"
	base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1"
	wire_api = "responses"

${PKGSTEM} uses a lot of memory, it starts with 256Mb per agent/worker
thread, and you may need to bump your limits (`ulimit -d`) or raise
datasize limit in used login class..

Also, ${PKGSTEM} may use a lot of open files, you may need to raise both
the system-wide maxfiles and used login class limits.

The local app-server daemon uses ${LOCALBASE}/bin/codex from this package.
It starts automatically for eligible interactive sessions, or explicitly with:

	codex app-server daemon start
	codex app-server daemon version
	codex app-server daemon stop

Use --no-daemon for an embedded session. Daemon sockets, PID records, logs,
and settings remain under CODEX_HOME (default ~/.codex); no daemon executable
or package manifest is installed there. The daemon inherits the environment
of the terminal that starts it, shared by later clients.

Daemon binaries are updated with pkg_add -u. Restart a running daemon with
`codex app-server daemon restart` after updating the package; a running process
continues using its old image until restarted. The daemon's internal updater
and --from-cli package installation are disabled for this port.

When migrating from an existing internally managed daemon, stop it with the
old CLI before replacing the package. No user-managed package files are deleted.
There is no system rc.d service: the daemon runs as the invoking user and is
not automatically restarted after reboot.
